@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Creating Repositories";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Repositories in Azure DevOps allow teams to store, manage, and track changes in their source code efficiently. Azure Repos supports both **Git repositories** (distributed version control) and **TFVC repositories** (centralized version control).</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Step-by-Step Guide to Creating a Repository in Azure Repos</h2>
        <p>Follow these steps to create a new repository in Azure DevOps:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 1: Navigate to Azure Repos</h4>
            <p>Sign in to <a href="https://dev.azure.com/" target="_blank">Azure DevOps</a>, select your project, and go to **Repos** in the left-hand menu.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/repo-navigation.png" alt="Navigating to Azure Repos" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Accessing Azure Repos</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/create-new-repo.png" alt="Creating a New Repository" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Creating a new repository</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 2: Create a New Repository</h4>
            <p>Click on **New Repository**, enter a name for your repository, choose either **Git** or **TFVC**, and click **Create**.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 3: Clone the Repository</h4>
            <p>To start working with your repository locally, clone it using **Git** by copying the repository URL and running:</p>
            <pre><code>git clone &lt;repository-url&gt;</code></pre>
            <p>Alternatively, you can use **Visual Studio, VS Code, or GitHub Desktop** for cloning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/git-clone.png" alt="Cloning a Repository" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Cloning a repository locally</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Managing Repositories</h2>
        <p>Once your repository is created, you can manage it efficiently using Azure Repos features:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Branching:</strong> Create and manage branches for different features and bug fixes.</li>
            <li><strong>Pull Requests:</strong> Submit code changes for review before merging.</li>
            <li><strong>Commit History:</strong> Track all changes made in the repository.</li>
            <li><strong>Permissions:</strong> Assign role-based access controls to manage who can modify the code.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Creating a repository in Azure DevOps is the first step in managing your source code effectively. By leveraging features like **branching, pull requests, and access control**, teams can collaborate seamlessly and maintain high code quality.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to learn about **Cloning, Branching, and Merging** in Azure Repos.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("GitVsTFVC", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: Git vs TFVC</a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("CloningBranchingMerging", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">Next: Cloning, Branching, and Merging &rarr;</a>
    </div>
</div>
